        "innocentReading": "The January 2018 changes were routine market-access relief. Investor counts in open-ended funds rise and fall, and institutional investors such as SWFs, pension funds and insurers are already regulated. So SEBI treated them like banks when deciding whether a fund is broad-based, and gave funds a cure period instead of automatic reclassification. It was proposed in 2017, long before any Adani allegation. The broad-based label is a registration category, not a beneficial-ownership rule: UBO disclosure under PMLA Rule 9 applied either way.",
