        "denialFound": "Yes, and it is recorded. OCCRP told ThePrint (7 Dec 2024): \"The U.S. government, while providing some funding to OCCRP, has no say in our editorial processes and no control over our reporting.\" The US State Department spokesperson said: \"It's disappointing that the ruling party in India would make these kinds of accusations\" (https://theprint.in/diplomacy/attempt-to-destabilise-india-bjp-claims-us-state-dept-targeting-country-with-adani-indictment/2392501/). OCCRP's FAQ (updated 7 Dec 2024) says: \"Donors never have access to editorial meetings or any aspect of our editorial process\". On key personnel it says \"a disagreement with a donor over a grant's 'key personnel' has never taken place\". The OCCRP board, quoted in Drop Site, says \"no donor has ever vetoed any staff position\".",
        "innocentReading": "OCCRP took US grants, like most of its peer outlets, because regional philanthropy is thin, and it publishes its funders. USAID's key-personnel approval is a standard term in US cooperative agreements, not editorial control. The Adani story was built on bank and fund records shared with the Guardian and the FT. The US indictment came from a separate DOJ/SEC process on a different subject. The BJP statement is ordinary partisan counter-framing after Congress used OCCRP and indictment material against the government.",
        "reason": "The core record holds. ThePrint confirms the BJP allegation (date, words, the \"50%\" citation). Drop Site confirms the funding figures word for word: \"Between 2014 and 2023, the American federal government provided 52 percent of the money actually spent by OCCRP\"; 46.4% on OCCRP's basis; 63% in 2016; \"at least $47 million\" since 2008; Gaydosik's quote on key personnel; and the board's no-veto statement. The narrative correctly separates dependency, which is evidenced, from direction, which is not, and a 'contested' status with 'alleged' as the underlying tier fits. What stops a clean pass is two defects. (1) The claim says the funding dependency is \"documented (52%)\", but the file's own edge c016 tiers it 'reported': it rests on a single journalist consortium's analysis of OCCRP's financials, not a primary audited filing. (2) strongestCounter says the US is India's \"largest defence and technology partner\". SIPRI's March 2025 fact sheet contradicts this: India's main suppliers in 2020–24 were \"Russia 36 France 33 Israel 13\", so the US is not in the top three. The sentence is also argument rather than evidence. The State Department's quoted words are \"disappointing that the ruling party in India would make these kinds of accusations\". That is a rebuke, not the explicit denial of editorial influence the file paraphrases.",
